{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The cerebellum is an infratentorial part of the brain in the posterior cranial fossa, made of two hemispheres joined by a midline vermis. It does not produce muscle power. It coordinates voluntary movement, adjusting its rate, range and force. Three pairs of peduncles connect it to the brainstem, and three pairs of arteries supply it. Damage causes ataxia.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="AN60.3, AN64.1" phase="Phase 1" /%}

## What is the cerebellum?

The cerebellum is an **infratentorial** structure, which means it lies below the tentorium cerebelli, in the **posterior cranial fossa** behind the pons and medulla oblongata. It is attached to the brainstem by three pairs of peduncles, and nerve fibres enter and leave through them.

It has two large lateral **hemispheres** joined by a narrow midline part called the **vermis**. Shallow grooves separate the vermis from each hemisphere. The surface is cut by many sulci and fissures into lobes, lobules and **folia** (leaflets).

The cerebellum is sometimes called a "silent area" of the brain. Electrical stimulation of it causes no conscious sensation and rarely causes any movement. Yet removing it makes body movements highly abnormal.

## Lobes and fissures

The **primary fissure** is the deepest fissure on a sagittal section. It divides the cerebellum into an **anterior lobe** and a **posterior lobe**. A third lobe, the **flocculonodular lobe**, lies inferiorly and is separated from the rest by the **posterior fissure**.

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/cerebellum/cerebellum-lobes-fissures.webp" alt="Cerebellum showing its lobes, fissures and vermis." caption="The cerebellum is divided into three lobes by two main fissures." credit="Original file created by OpenStax College, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1613_Major_Regions_of_the_Cerebellum-02.svg" width="960" height="499" /%}

The same three parts can be named by their evolutionary age:

{% table %}
- **Lobe**
- **Older name**
- **Key points**
---
- **Flocculonodular lobe**
- Archicerebellum
- Lies inferiorly. It is the oldest part in evolution. It is linked to the vestibular nuclei and helps maintain posture.
---
- **Anterior lobe**
- Paleocerebellum
- Lies rostral to the primary fissure. In lower animals it forms most of the cerebellum, but in humans it is relatively small. It consists of the anterosuperior vermis and the paravermian cortex next to it.
---
- **Posterior lobe**
- Neocerebellum
- Made of the middle parts of the vermis and their large lateral extensions. The cerebellar hemispheres, the major part of the cerebellum, fall in this division.
{% /table %}

## Development in brief

The cerebellum develops from the **rhombic lip**, the dorsal part of the alar plate of the metencephalon. This lip forms the rostral margin of the diamond-shaped fourth ventricle. Two rounded swellings appear and form the rudimentary hemispheres. Cells from the alar plate invade the roof between them and form the rudiment of the vermis. Later, transverse grooves appear on the surface. These are the precursors of the fissures of the mature cerebellum.

## Microanatomy (AN64.1)

The cerebellar cortex is a large folded sheet, about 17 cm wide by 120 cm long. The folds lie crosswise, and each fold is a **folium**. A histological section of the folia shows **three cortical layers** over white matter. Deep in the white matter lie the deep cerebellar nuclei.

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/cerebellum/cerebellar-cortex-layers.webp" alt="Diagram of the three layers of the cerebellar cortex and their cells." caption="Purkinje cells carry the only output from the cerebellar cortex." credit="Tom282f3, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cerebellar_Circuit.svg" width="960" height="835" /%}

- The **molecular layer** is the outermost layer.
- **Purkinje cells** lie at the junction of the molecular and granular layers. They are the characteristic cell of the cerebellar slide.
- The **granular layer** is the deepest cortical layer.

The axons of Purkinje cells are the efferent fibres of the cerebellar cortex. They project onto the deep cerebellar nuclei, and their effect is **inhibitory**. Granule cell axons release the excitatory transmitter glutamate. All the inhibitory neurons of the cerebellar cortex appear to use GABA. The deep nuclei, in turn, send excitatory fibres to other parts of the motor system.

## Deep cerebellar nuclei

There are four pairs of nuclei in the white matter:

1. **Dentate** nucleus: neocerebellar
2. **Globose** nucleus
3. **Emboliform** nucleus: paleocerebellar
4. **Fastigial** nucleus: archicerebellar

In humans the globose and emboliform nuclei are combined and called the **interposed nucleus**.

## Functional zones and their connections

The cerebellum can also be divided by function into zones. Each zone projects to its own deep nucleus.

{% table %}
- **Zone**
- **Main role**
- **Projects to**
---
- **Vermis**
- Control of muscles of the axial body, neck, shoulders and hips. The axial parts of the body are represented here.
- Fastigial nucleus
---
- **Intermediate zone** (of the hemisphere)
- Control of muscle contraction in the distal limbs, especially hands, fingers, feet and toes. It influences postural tone and movements of the ipsilateral limbs. The limbs and face are represented here.
- Globose and emboliform nuclei (interposed nucleus)
---
- **Lateral zone** (of the hemisphere)
- Joins the cerebral cortex in planning and coordinating sequential motor movements. Without it, motor acts lose their timing and sequence and become uncoordinated.
- Dentate nucleus
{% /table %}

The functional parts are also named **vestibulocerebellum**, **spinocerebellum** and **cerebrocerebellum**. The spinocerebellum receives tactile, proprioceptive, auditory and visual impulses. The cerebrocerebellum projects to the dentate nucleus, and from there directly or indirectly to the ventrolateral nucleus of the thalamus.

A body map in the cerebellar cortex reflects **sensory**, not motor, organisation. It maps peripheral sensory stimuli to the cerebellar cortex.

## Cerebellar peduncles

Three pairs of peduncles connect the cerebellum with the brainstem. They do not each connect to one single level, but in general:

- The **inferior** peduncle connects at the level of the medulla.
- The **middle** peduncle connects at mid-pons.
- The **superior** peduncle connects at the upper pons.

{% table %}
- **Peduncle**
- **Afferent (into the cerebellum)**
- **Efferent (out of the cerebellum)**
---
- **Superior** (brachium conjunctivum)
- Anterior (ventral) spinocerebellar tract, tectocerebellar tract
- The principal efferent fibres of the cerebellum: dentatorubral and dentatothalamic fibres, also cerebellorubral, cerebelloreticular, cerebellotegmental and cerebellotectal fibres, and cerebellovestibular fibres in the uncinate fasciculus
---
- **Middle** (brachium pontis)
- Pontocerebellar fibres from the contralateral basal pontine nuclei
- None
---
- **Inferior**
- Restiform body: posterior (dorsal) spinocerebellar, cuneocerebellar, olivocerebellar, trigeminocerebellar, reticulocerebellar fibres and external arcuate fibres
- Juxtarestiform body: mainly efferent, Purkinje cell axons to the vestibular nuclei and uncrossed fibres from the fastigial nucleus
{% /table %}

Key points about each peduncle:

- The **middle peduncle** is the most lateral and by far the largest. It carries the pontocerebellar mossy fibre pathway. These are the final neurons of the corticopontocerebellar pathway, which comes mainly from the frontal, temporal and other cortical areas to the contralateral cerebellar hemisphere.
- The **inferior peduncle** lies medial to the middle peduncle. Its outer part, the **restiform body**, is a purely afferent system. Its medial part, the **juxtarestiform body**, is mainly efferent. It also carries afferent fibres from the vestibular nerve and from the vestibular nuclei to the cerebellum.
- The **superior peduncle** fibres from the dentate and interposed nuclei enter the upper pontine tegmentum, cross (decussate) at the level of the inferior colliculus and ascend to the ventrolateral nucleus of the thalamus.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Peduncle memory aid:** the middle peduncle brings the pons in (pontocerebellar, afferent only). The superior peduncle is the main route out (dentatothalamic, dentatorubral). The inferior peduncle brings in spinal, olivary and other medullary fibres.
{% /callout %}

## Inputs to the cerebellum

- **From the cerebral cortex:** the corticopontocerebellar pathway starts in the motor, premotor and somatosensory cortex. It passes through the pontine nuclei and pontocerebellar tracts, mainly to the lateral parts of the **opposite** cerebellar hemisphere.
- **From the inferior olive:** the olivocerebellar tract goes to all parts of the cerebellum. The olive is excited by fibres from the motor cortex, basal ganglia, reticular formation and spinal cord.
- **From the vestibular system:** vestibulocerebellar fibres end almost entirely in the flocculonodular lobe and fastigial nucleus.
- **From the reticular formation:** reticulocerebellar fibres end mainly in the vermis.
- **From the periphery:** the dorsal spinocerebellar tract enters through the inferior peduncle and ends in the vermis and intermediate zones on the same side as its origin. The ventral tract tells the cerebellum which motor signals have reached the anterior horns, a feedback called the **efference copy**. Spinocerebellar pathways conduct at up to 120 m/sec, the fastest conduction in any central nervous system pathway.

Because of these inputs the cerebellum continually collects information about the movement and position of all parts of the body, even though it works at a subconscious level.

## Outputs and the main motor circuits

- **Lateral zone circuit:** cerebellar cortex of the lateral zone, then the dentate nucleus, then the ventrolateral and ventroanterior nuclei of the thalamus, then the cerebral cortex. This circuit helps coordinate sequential motor activities started by the cerebral cortex.
- **Intermediate zone circuit:** intermediate zone, then the interposed nucleus, then the ventrolateral and ventroanterior thalamus and cortex, with further connections to the red nucleus and reticular formation of the brainstem. This circuit helps coordinate the reciprocal contraction of agonist and antagonist muscles in the distal limbs, especially hands and fingers.
- **Ipsilateral control:** the circuit from cerebellar cortex to dentate nucleus to ventral thalamus to motor cortex links the cerebellum to voluntary movement of the **ipsilateral** limbs. For example, when the right fingers move repeatedly, functional imaging shows activity in the left precentral gyrus and the right cerebellar hemisphere.

## What does the cerebellum do?

The cerebellum brings finesse to the motor system. It is not primarily involved in producing muscle power. Instead, it is needed for the normal control and regulation of muscle contraction. Its main clinical role is the **coordination of movement**.

- It receives the desired sequence of muscle contractions from the motor control areas, and also continuous sensory information from the body. It compares the two and makes corrective adjustments while a movement is under way.
- To perform a movement, the agonists contract, the antagonists relax or change their tone, the synergists reinforce the movement, and the fixating muscles hold the posture of the limb. The cerebellum coordinates all four.
- It is especially vital in rapid activities such as running, typing, playing the piano and talking.
- Its lesions do not cause weakness. They cause loss of coordination and an inability to judge and regulate the **rate, range and force** of movement.
- None of its motor activity reaches conscious perception.
- In a simple model, the deep nucleus gives a "turn on" signal to start a movement. Purkinje cells later send a strong inhibitory signal to the same nuclear cell, which helps "turn off" the movement after a short, precise time.

## Blood supply (AN60.3)

The cerebellum is supplied by **three pairs of arteries** from the vertebrobasilar system. They are circumferential arteries that wind around the brainstem. Each side has one of each:

{% table %}
- **Artery**
- **Origin**
- **Notes**
---
- **Superior cerebellar (SCA)**
- Basilar artery
- Supplies the superior cerebellum
---
- **Anterior inferior cerebellar (AICA)**
- Basilar artery
- Supplies the ventral cerebellum
---
- **Posterior inferior cerebellar (PICA)**
- Usually the vertebral artery
- The largest branch of the vertebral artery. It supplies the posterolateral medulla, lower pons and the inferior surface of the cerebellum, with choroidal branches to the fourth ventricle.
{% /table %}

The SCA and AICA are the long circumferential branches of the basilar artery. They curve round the pons to supply the cerebellar hemispheres. The cerebellar arteries form superficial anastomoses on the cortical surface.

**Venous drainage:** the cerebellar veins run on the surface in superior and inferior groups.

- **Superior veins** run to the straight sinus or great cerebral vein, or laterally to the transverse and superior petrosal sinuses.
- **Inferior veins** include a small median vein that runs back over the inferior vermis to the straight or sigmoid sinus. Laterally running veins join the inferior petrosal and occipital sinuses.

## Cerebellar dysfunction: signs and tests (AN60.3)

Damage to the cerebellum causes **hypotonia**, **ataxia** (uncoordinated movement) and **intention tremor**. Which signs appear depends on the region involved.

Without the cerebellum, the subconscious motor system cannot predict how far a movement will go. The movement overshoots, then the conscious brain overcorrects the other way. This is **dysmetria**, and the resulting clumsy movement is ataxia.

{% table %}
- **Sign**
- **What it means**
- **How to test**
---
- **Intention tremor**
- A tremor only during movement, which becomes coarser and more irregular as the target is approached
- Finger-nose test, heel-knee test
---
- **Dysmetria**
- Misjudging the range of a movement, by stopping short or overshooting (past pointing)
- Finger-nose test
---
- **Dyssynergia (decomposition)**
- Inability to carry out a movement as a smooth, coordinated sequence. It breaks into small, jerky parts.
- Finger-nose test
---
- **Dysdiadochokinesia**
- Inability to do rapid alternating movements smoothly. Movements are slow, clumsy and incomplete, on the side of the lesion.
- Rapid pronation and supination of the forearm
---
- **Rebound phenomenon**
- After sudden release of resistance, the limb overshoots, and the hand may strike the face. It is due to hypotonia, as the antagonist cannot contract promptly.
- Flex the elbow against resistance, then release suddenly
---
- **Nystagmus**
- To and fro oscillation of the eyes. In cerebellar disease it is horizontal and jerky, with the fast component towards the side of the lesion.
- Look to either side
---
- **Scanning speech**
- Jerky, explosive speech
- Listen to speech
---
- **Ataxic gait**
- Staggering, lurching, unsteady gait, steadied a little by a wide base
- Watch walking
{% /table %}

Other points:

- **Heel-knee-shin test:** the patient touches the heel of one foot to the opposite knee, then slides it down the shin and back. Do it supine to remove the effect of gravity.
- **Romberg's sign** is a test of **sensory** ataxia. In cerebellar disease it is negative: unsteadiness does not worsen when the eyes are closed. Cerebellar ataxia is not improved by vision. Ataxia from posterior column disease worsens with eyes closed.
- **Finger to finger test:** in a unilateral cerebellar lesion the arm on the involved side sags and undershoots.
- **Cerebellar cognitive affective syndrome** can follow thrombosis of one of the six arteries supplying the cerebellum. Patients show inattention, grammatical errors in speech and patchy memory loss.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**DANISH:** **D**ysdiadochokinesia, **A**taxic gait, **N**ystagmus, **I**ntention tremor, **S**canning speech, **H**ypotonia (and heel-shin test).
{% /callout %}

## Lesions by region (AN60.3)

{% table %}
- **Site of lesion**
- **Effects**
---
- **Vermis (midline)**
- Truncal and gait ataxia, because its connections to the vestibular nuclei are involved. The patient sways with eyes open and falls in any direction, especially forwards or backwards. Nystagmus occurs through loss of labyrinthine connections. The vermis is also related to emotion.
---
- **Anterior lobe**
- Gait ataxia: incoordination of the lower limbs with a staggering gait and inability to walk in a straight line. This is seen in alcoholics (rostral vermis syndrome).
---
- **Flocculonodular lobe (caudal vermis)**
- Axial disequilibrium and staggering gait, with little or no limb ataxia. Spontaneous nystagmus may occur. Tumours such as medulloblastoma are an example.
---
- **Cerebellar hemisphere**
- Classic ipsilateral limb ataxia: intention tremor, past pointing and mild hypotonia. The patient deviates or falls towards the side of the lesion. Infarcts, neoplasms and abscesses are examples.
---
- **Neocerebellum**
- Incoordination of voluntary movement of the upper limbs, with intention tremor, action tremor and overshoot. Speech is also defective: phonation loses smoothness and articulation is poorly coordinated.
---
- **Whole cerebellum (pancerebellar)**
- Bilateral signs involving the trunk, limbs and cranial muscles. Causes include infections, hypoglycaemia and toxic-metabolic disorders.
{% /table %}

Good to know: destroying a small part of the lateral cortex seldom causes visible abnormality, because the rest of the motor system compensates. Serious, lasting dysfunction usually needs a lesion that involves one or more of the deep nuclei: dentate, interposed or fastigial.

## Stroke and herniation

**Cerebellar strokes**

- **PICA territory, medial:** vermis and vestibulocerebellum are involved. There is prominent vertigo, ataxia and nystagmus.
- **PICA territory, lateral hemisphere:** vertigo, nausea, vomiting, gait ataxia, limb dysmetria, gaze palsies, miosis and dysarthria.
- **AICA occlusion:** ventral cerebellar infarction, with vertigo, nausea, vomiting and nystagmus from involvement of the vestibular nuclei. There is also ipsilateral facial loss of pain and temperature sensation and reduced corneal sensation, because the trigeminal spinal nucleus and tract are involved.

**Herniation:** the posterior fossa is a small compartment. A space-occupying lesion there can push the cerebellar vermis upward through the tentorial hiatus (upward transtentorial herniation). It can also push the cerebellar tonsils downward through the foramen magnum (**tonsillar herniation**). Tonsillar herniation compresses the brainstem and is life-threatening. These are neurosurgical emergencies that need a suboccipital craniectomy. Signs of tonsillar herniation include neck pain, stiff neck, head tilt and arching of the neck.

**Chiari malformation:** a group of disorders of the posterior fossa and hindbrain. In **type I**, the least severe, the cerebellar tonsils protrude through the foramen magnum into the upper cervical canal, and may be found by chance. In **type II**, the medulla, inferior vermis and tonsils are displaced downward, with hydrocephalus. A lumbar meningomyelocele is common.

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="Cerebellar lesions do not cause weakness. Muscle power is preserved, but the patient cannot gauge and regulate the rate, range and force of movement, so movements are clumsy and uncoordinated." %}
The characteristic effect of a cerebellar lesion is:
- A. Paralysis of the muscles of the same side
- B. Loss of conscious sensation from the limbs
- C. Loss of coordination with preserved muscle power
- D. Loss of muscle tone with increased power
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The middle cerebellar peduncle is the most lateral and by far the largest. It carries the massive pontocerebellar mossy fibre pathway." %}
Which cerebellar peduncle is the largest?
- A. Superior
- B. Middle
- C. Inferior
- D. All are equal in size
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="The anterior spinocerebellar tract enters the cerebellum through the superior peduncle. Posterior spinocerebellar and olivocerebellar fibres enter through the inferior peduncle, and vestibulocerebellar fibres through its juxtarestiform part." %}
Which of these fibres travels in the superior cerebellar peduncle?
- A. Posterior spinocerebellar
- B. Olivocerebellar
- C. Vestibulocerebellar
- D. Anterior spinocerebellar
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="The superior cerebellar and anterior inferior cerebellar arteries are both branches of the basilar artery. The posterior inferior cerebellar artery usually arises from the vertebral artery." %}
Which pair of cerebellar arteries arises from the basilar artery?
- A. Superior cerebellar and anterior inferior cerebellar
- B. Superior cerebellar and posterior inferior cerebellar
- C. Anterior inferior cerebellar and posterior inferior cerebellar
- D. Posterior inferior cerebellar only
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Little or no change in unsteadiness with eye closure (Romberg negative) suggests a motor ataxia, with possible localisation to the cerebellum. Worsening with eye closure suggests a sensory ataxia from loss of proprioception." %}
A patient with ataxia is no more unsteady when the eyes are closed. This most suggests:
- A. Sensory ataxia from posterior column disease
- B. Motor ataxia, possibly from a cerebellar lesion
- C. A normal person
- D. Peripheral neuropathy
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="Purkinje cell axons are the efferent fibres of the cerebellar cortex and they inhibit the deep cerebellar nuclei. The deep nuclei, in turn, excite other parts of the motor system." %}
The output of the Purkinje cells to the deep cerebellar nuclei is:
- A. Inhibitory
- B. Excitatory
- C. Both excitatory and inhibitory in equal measure
- D. Not directed to the deep nuclei
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="Where is the cerebellum and what are its parts?" %}
It is an infratentorial structure in the posterior cranial fossa, behind the pons and medulla. It has two hemispheres joined by a midline vermis. It has three lobes: anterior, posterior and flocculonodular.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the deep cerebellar nuclei." %}
Dentate, globose, emboliform and fastigial. In humans the globose and emboliform nuclei are combined as the interposed nucleus.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the three peduncles and the level of the brainstem each connects to." %}
Inferior peduncle to the medulla, middle peduncle to the mid-pons, and superior peduncle to the upper pons.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which peduncle carries the main efferent fibres, and where do they go?" %}
The superior peduncle. Dentatorubral and dentatothalamic fibres cross at the level of the inferior colliculus and ascend to the red nucleus and the ventrolateral nucleus of the thalamus.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Give the blood supply of the cerebellum." %}
Three pairs of arteries from the vertebrobasilar system: the superior cerebellar and anterior inferior cerebellar arteries from the basilar artery, and the posterior inferior cerebellar artery usually from the vertebral artery.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the three functional zones and their roles." %}
The vermis controls the axial body, neck, shoulders and hips. The intermediate zone controls the distal limbs. The lateral zone helps plan sequential voluntary movements with the cerebral cortex.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Why does a cerebellar lesion cause ataxia but not paralysis?" %}
The cerebellum does not produce muscle contraction. It coordinates and regulates the rate, range and force of movement made by the motor system. Muscle power is preserved, but movements become uncoordinated.
{% /viva %}

